    l.sort(); // O(N log N)
}

output:
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

void sort_vector(std::vector<int>& v) {
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
}
---
Suggesting vector over list for sorting. While list::sort is O(N log N), sorting a vector is significantly faster in practice due to cache locality. If the list is large, it's often faster to copy to a vector, sort, and copy back.

